---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfSecureUnlock from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfSecureUnlock.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="unlock-pdf">
  <PdfSecureUnlock client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Two kinds of locked PDF</h2>
      <p>
        "Locked" can mean two technically different things. The unlock PDF tool tells you which one you have
        before you start.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col">Restricted (owner password only)</th>
              <th scope="col">Encrypted (user password)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">What you see</th>
              <td>It opens normally, but printing, copying or editing is greyed out</td>
              <td>A password box appears before anything is shown</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Password needed here</th>
              <td>None</td>
              <td>Yes, the one you were given</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Why it can be removed</th>
              <td>
                The content is encrypted with an empty password, so anyone can derive the key
              </td>
              <td>The key is derived from your password, so you must supply it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Typical source</th>
              <td>Bank statements, e-books, tender documents, form templates</td>
              <td>Payslips, medical results, legal discovery, HR letters</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How the lossless method works</h2>
      <p>
        Re-printing a PDF destroys its text layer. This tool tries a structural rewrite first:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          It parses the file into its object graph. Encryption hides only the strings and stream data, not the
          structure, so the catalog, page tree and cross-reference entries are readable from the start.
        </li>
        <li>
          It reads the <code>/Encrypt</code> dictionary to find the revision, cipher and key length, and checks
          your password against the stored <code>/U</code> or <code>/O</code> hash. A wrong password fails here,
          before anything is written.
        </li>
        <li>
          It decrypts, inflates and expands the compressed object streams, which a modern PDF uses to pack most
          of its small objects, so their contents can be reached.
        </li>
        <li>
          It decrypts each remaining string and stream with its per-object key (revisions 2–4) or the file key
          (revisions 5–6), and deletes the <code>/Encrypt</code> entry from the trailer.
        </li>
        <li>
          It writes the result, then <strong>opens and renders it again</strong> as a check. Only if that check
          fails does the tool rebuild the pages as images, and the result card tells you so.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        With the lossless method, text stays selectable and searchable, links work, bookmarks stay, and form
        fields stay fillable.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Supported PDF encryption schemes</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Revision</th>
              <th scope="col">Cipher</th>
              <th scope="col">Introduced with</th>
              <th scope="col">Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>2</td>
              <td>RC4, 40-bit</td>
              <td>PDF 1.1</td>
              <td>Easily broken by modern hardware; still found in old archives</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>3</td>
              <td>RC4, 40–128-bit</td>
              <td>PDF 1.4</td>
              <td>Adds the 50-round MD5 key strengthening</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>4</td>
              <td>RC4 or AES-128</td>
              <td>PDF 1.5 / 1.6</td>
              <td>Adds crypt filters, so streams and strings can differ</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>5</td>
              <td>AES-256</td>
              <td>PDF 1.7 extension level 3</td>
              <td>Deprecated: a single SHA-256, weak against fast guessing</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>6</td>
              <td>AES-256</td>
              <td>PDF 1.7 extension level 8 / ISO 32000-2</td>
              <td>The current scheme, with the iterated Algorithm 2.B hash</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Non-standard handlers are not supported: Adobe Experience Manager rights management, FileOpen,
        Locklizard and similar enterprise DRM replace the security handler and need their licence server. The
        tool identifies those files by name and refuses them, so they are not damaged.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>No password cracking</h2>
      <p>
        To recover an unknown password, you must guess it. For revision 6, each guess costs at least
        sixty-four rounds of SHA-2 plus AES over a 64× expanded buffer, by design of the specification. A
        browser tab can try a few thousand candidates per second; a rented GPU cluster is much faster but still
        needs centuries for a good passphrase.
      </p>
      <p>
        Password recovery services either try only a dictionary of common passwords or need your file on their
        server. This tool does neither.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>If the password is lost</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Ask the sender. Banks, payroll providers and government agencies use a formula: a date of birth, a
          national insurance or PAN number, the last four digits of an account, sometimes a surname in capitals.
          The covering email usually explains it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Check your password manager and browser autofill. Document passwords are often saved with the portal
          they came from.
        </li>
        <li>
          Try obvious variants. Case matters, and so do trailing spaces copied from an email and a keyboard
          layout that typed a different character.
        </li>
        <li>
          If you can still open the file somewhere, for example on a phone that remembered the password, print
          it to PDF there to get an unrestricted copy.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Legal and ethical use</h2>
      <p>
        This page cannot tell whether the document is your own payslip or another person's contract, because
        it never sees the document. It is normal to remove protection from a file you were given, so you can
        read it on a device that has problems with the password prompt or copy a quote from it. To bypass
        protection on material you were not meant to have is not, and in many jurisdictions it is unlawful,
        however easy the tool makes it. You decide.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>After unlocking</h2>
      <p>
        A decrypted PDF is an ordinary PDF, so you can use the other PDF tools: <a href="/merge-pdf"
          >merge it</a
        > with other documents, <a href="/split-pdf">split out</a> the pages you need,
        <a href="/compress-pdf">compress it</a> for email, or
        <a href="/pdf-to-text">extract the text</a>. To protect it again with your own password,
        <a href="/password-protect-pdf">Password Protect PDF</a> writes AES-256 in the same tab.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
